 1.2–1.25 × forecast. The month ends with 140 or more unsold units, and the warehouse overflows.\n\n**Ordering every three days (candidate 1).** Both products arrive on the same days in lots of about 110–160 each. Together they exceed the 224-unit limit again and again, worst on days 17 and 20.\n\n**Daily ordering (candidates 3 and 4).** Ordering every day costs 260 more in daily order fees, about 560 against 300. It saves about 315 in holding costs, because a second day's stock no longer sits in the warehouse overnight. However, shortages are checked every day instead of every second day, which loses about 3 more units of each product. Candidate 4 also orders product 0 at about 1.10 × forecast. Candidate 3 orders 1.2 and 1.15 times forecast, which leaves a large surplus.\n\n**Warehouse limit around the demand spikes.** Demand spikes on day 20 (product 0, 92) and day 21 (product 1, 99).\n- **Candidate 6** overflows by about 29 units of product 0 on day 19 and about 15 units of product 1 on day 21. I estimate this costs around 270.\n- **Candidate 0** keeps less stock and overflows almost not at all, costing about 40.\n- **Candidate 4** loses about 30 units of product 1 on days 20–21.\n\n**Estimated totals.** Candidate 6 beats candidate 0 by roughly 250 on ordering level. It gives back roughly 230 of that to the warehouse limit, so it is ahead by about 20–50. Candidate 4 trails candidate 6 by about 50–100. These gaps are smaller than the error in my estimates."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 6138, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 6138}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T06:44:23.564Z", 